<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Go Shopping! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want Five Pineapples.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Organize information from discussions into written form.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences about shopping (e.g., "I want bananas."). Can organize information from discussions about shopping into written form (e.g., "I need apples and oranges."). Can write simple phrases and sentences about shopping (e.g., "Three apples, two bananas.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of fruit (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) and numbers (one to five). Students chorally repeat the names and numbers. Teacher asks, "What do you want to buy?" and points to flashcards, encouraging students to say the words. This reactivates vocabulary from previous lessons, preparing for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes on the board, "I want three apples." The teacher clearly pronounces "apples" (/æpəlz/) and "three" (/θriː/), emphasizing the /p/ sound in 'apple'. The teacher circles "three" and "apples," explaining that "three" is a number and "apples" is a fruit. Then, the teacher writes, "I want two bananas, please." The teacher pronounces "bananas" (/bənɑːnəz/), highlighting the /b/ sound. The teacher explains "please" for being polite. The teacher models writing the sentence clearly and slowly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to "apples" written on the board) "Is this fruit? Yes or no?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to "three") "Is this a color or a number?" (Expected answer: "A number!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'please,' am I polite or rude?" (Expected answer: "Polite!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated shopping list showing different quantities of fruits (e.g., "Shopping List: 1 pineapple, 4 oranges, 2 bananas, 5 apples"). This serves as a visual and written input for students to draw inspiration for their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student uses simple words and phrases to express what they want or need while shopping, using polite forms. Students will identify the structure of a simple written request (Number + Noun).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher displays the shopping list on the board or as a handout.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this? Is it a story or a list?" (Expected answer: "A list!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students draw lines from numbers (1, 2, 4, 5) to the correct fruit picture shown next to the text on their handout. E.g., match "1" to a picture of a pineapple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information identification:</b> Teacher asks, "How many oranges are on the list?" Students point to "4 oranges" on the list and say "Four."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using "Number + noun" and "Present simple (I want...)" with target vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) and polite expressions ("please"). Phonemic awareness focus on blending sounds in words like "six" and "money" when writing numbers and quantities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze activity for writing):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences and picture prompts. For example: "I want ___ (picture of 3 apples)." Students write the number "three" and the word "apples" to complete the sentence. Another example: "I need five ___ (picture of 5 bananas)." Students fill in "bananas." The teacher guides by pointing to the pictures and eliciting the words orally before students write.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Building with Word Bank):</b> The teacher provides a list of numbers (one, two, three, four, five, ten) and a list of fruits (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money). Students are instructed to write five sentences about what they want to buy, choosing one number and one fruit from the lists. They must start each sentence with "I want..." and include "please" at the end for at least two sentences. Example: "I want four oranges, please." Teacher reminds students of polite expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Practice (Peer Check & Dictation):</b> In pairs, one student (Student A) draws a simple shopping list with 2-3 items (e.g., "2 apples, 1 banana"). Student A then dictates their list to Student B, saying each item and quantity clearly. Student B writes it down. Then they switch roles. Students then check each other's written lists for correct numbers and fruit names. This activity promotes both listening and writing, with an immediate peer check for accuracy in writing the target vocabulary and numbers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "My Shopping List for the Market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students imagine they are going to the market or a store. They need to write their own shopping list of items they want to buy. They must choose at least five different items (fruits) and specify the quantity for each, using numbers from one to ten. Students are encouraged to use the phrase "I want..." for each item and "please" for at least two requests, incorporating polite expressions learned. The teacher circulates, providing support with vocabulary and spelling as needed, focusing on legible writing and correct use of numbers and fruit names. Students should use vocabulary like pineapple, apple, orange, banana, and numbers. For example: "I want five pineapples, please. I want ten oranges. I want two bananas. I want three fresh apples. I need money."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This activity directly relates to "I Want Five Pineapples" as students produce their own version of a shopping list, requesting items and quantities, thereby demonstrating their ability to write short texts about shopping.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and rubric for the written shopping list.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Shopping List for the Market" written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric adapted for 3rd Grade A1.1 Writing):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Writes at least 5 different fruit names correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Numbers:</b> Writes numbers (1-10) correctly with items.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Structure:</b> Uses "I want [number] [fruit]" or "[number] [fruit]" for at least 3 items.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Polite Expressions:</b> Includes "please" in at least one request.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Writing is generally clear and easy to read.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket by writing "I can write about shopping for ____ and ____." (e.g., "I can write about shopping for apples and bananas.") and "My writing is (good/okay/needs practice)."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to write about today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What words or numbers were difficult to write?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the writing activities? Were students able to compose simple sentences about shopping effectively? Which grammar point (number + noun, present simple) or vocabulary item needs more reinforcement in writing? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence starters, visual dictionaries) are needed for students who struggled with writing the quantity and items? How can I further integrate phonemic awareness, like blending of /s-ɪ-k-s/ in "six," into writing practice?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on individual writing. The next lesson (Lesson 5) will build on this by having students share their shopping lists and create a class "market" inventory, requiring them to mediate and integrate information from various sources into a larger project, further solidifying their understanding of shopping vocabulary and expressions in a real-world context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>